# 97-rpi0-logger-net.preset — turn on the WiFi supplicant for wlan0.
#
# WHY A PRESET FILE AND NOT A BBAPPEND
#
# The unit we want enabled, wpa_supplicant@.service, is shipped by poky's
# wpa-supplicant recipe, which deliberately sets
# SYSTEMD_AUTO_ENABLE = "disable" — sensible, since a supplicant with no
# config is useless and only the integrator knows the interface name.
#
# The obvious move is a wpa-supplicant_%.bbappend setting
# SYSTEMD_SERVICE:${PN}:append:raspberrypi0-wifi = " wpa_supplicant@wlan0.service".
# That works, but it makes the CONTENT of a shared package depend on the
# machine while its PACKAGE_ARCH stays the tune arch — the sort of thing
# that only bites you the day two machines share a tune.
#
# The preset file is cleaner and completely self-contained. image.bbclass
# (line ~728) runs, on every systemd image:
#
#     systemctl --root=${IMAGE_ROOTFS} --preset-mode=enable-only preset-all
#
# as an IMAGE_PREPROCESS_COMMAND. That walks every preset file in the
# rootfs and applies it, so a file dropped here by ANY package is honoured
# at image-assembly time. It is exactly how systemd-networkd ends up
# enabled in our image: upstream's own 90-systemd.preset says so.
#
# Numbering: presets are read in lexical order and the FIRST matching rule
# for a unit wins. "97-" puts us ahead of poky's 98-* recipe-generated
# presets and well ahead of 99-default.preset, which is a blanket
# "disable *".
#
# NOTE THE UNIT NAME BELOW: the TEMPLATE, "wpa_supplicant@.service", not
# the instance "wpa_supplicant@wlan0.service". This file said the latter on
# its first outing and it silently did nothing — preset-all iterates over
# unit FILES, and an instance name is not a file. Which instance gets
# enabled is decided by DefaultInstance= in the drop-in this recipe also
# ships (10-default-instance.conf), where the full post-mortem lives.
#
# Two limits worth knowing before the optimize pass:
#
#   * "--preset-mode=enable-only" means preset files can only turn things
#     ON. A "disable foo.service" line here is silently ignored. When we
#     strip systemd-networkd-wait-online.service and friends, the tool is
#     masking or SYSTEMD_SERVICE surgery, not this file.
#
#   * The rootfs is assembled by systemd-systemctl-native — a Python
#     reimplementation of systemctl in poky, not the real thing. It is
#     close, but "the host's systemctl does X" is not evidence about the
#     build. Test against
#     poky/meta/recipes-core/systemd/systemd-systemctl/systemctl, which is
#     a script you can run against a throwaway --root.

enable wpa_supplicant@.service
